  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1026 PM MDT Tue Jun 9 2026\n\nAn elongated ridge running from Arizona to the central Gulf coast\nwill keep our temperatures above average through the end of the\nweek. High temperatures in the lowlands Wednesday and Thursday \nwill run 5 to 7 degrees above average. This is warm enough to \nwarrant a heat advisory for much of El Paso county and the Rio \nGrande Valley of Hudspeth county. The position of the upper level \nridge will make all the difference on our temperatures for Friday\nthrough Sunday. Right now it looks like the upper level ridge \nwill retreat to the east a bit and this will allow some moisture \nto slip back into the region. High temperature for this weekend \nwill drop back closer to normal with a chance for rain both \nafternoons. But if the ridge strengthens and remains in place, we \nwill stay dry and our triple digit heat will continue. For now I \nwill lean more toward the \"cooler\" solution, but we may need to \nadjust things if the ridge looks like it is anchored here a little\nlonger.\n\nFor the start of next week, an upper level trough moving through\nthe Midwest will squeeze the upper level ridge to our west and\nthat will keep us from getting quite so hot. But the extended\nmodels are indicating that the upper level ridge will build right\nback into the region for the middle and end of next week. So if \nwe do get a break from the 100 degree temperature it looks like it\nwill only be for a few days.",
